#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096307 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0f247351-8c81-595e-bb30-26630159819c" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Missing Coefficient"}] :question/id #uuid "97842ba7-b957-51da-9765-18cd94976e5a" :question/math-academy-id "e-12333" :question/problem "Given that\n$\\dfrac{7x+6}{(x+2)^2} = \\dfrac{A}{x+2} -\\dfrac{8}{(x+2)^2}$,\n\nwhat is the value of $A$?" :question/worked-solution "Multiplying both sides of the given equality by $(x+2)^2$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n7x + 6 &= A(x + 2) - 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can substitute some value for $x$ and solve for $A$.\n\nWe *can’t* choose $x=-2$, since this would eliminate $A$ from the equation. But we can choose any other value of $x$. So let’s choose a simple value, say, $x=0$.\n\nPlugging $x=0$ into the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n7x + 6 &= A(x + 2) - 8 \\\\\n7(0) + 6 &= A(0 + 2) - 8 \\\\\n6 &= 2A - 8 \\\\\n14 &= 2A \\\\\nA &= 7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096308 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e0d98b82-24d2-5b2c-b596-0300778bd187" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Two-Term Partial Fraction Decomposition"}] :question/id #uuid "84d82f4d-b725-5779-a570-cbb3e0e9e429" :question/math-academy-id "e-12334" :question/problem "Given that\n$\\dfrac{7-4x}{(x+5)^2} = \\dfrac{A}{x+5} +\\dfrac{B}{(x+5)^2}$,\n\nfind the values of $A$ and $B$." :question/worked-solution "Multiplying both sides of the given equality by $(x+5)^2$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n7 - 4x &= A(x + 5) + B\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to solve for $A$ and $B$, and both of these constants appear in the equation simultaneously. However, we can eliminate $A$ if we substitute $x=-5$.\n\nPlugging $x=-5$ into the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n7 - 4x &= A(x + 5) + B \\\\\n7 - 4(-5) &= A(-5 + 5) + B \\\\\n27 &= A(0) + B \\\\\n27 &= 0 + B \\\\\n27 &= B\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to solve for $A$. To do this, let’s start by substituting our value $B=27$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n7 - 4x &= A(x + 5) + 27\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can substitute some value for $x$ and solve for $A$.\n\nWe *can’t* choose $x=-5$, since this would eliminate $A$ from the equation. But we can choose any other value of $x$. So let’s choose a simple value, say, $x=0$.\n\nPlugging $x=0$ into the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n7 - 4x &= A(x + 5) + 27 \\\\\n7 - 4(0) &= A(0 + 5) + 27 \\\\\n7 &= 5A + 27 \\\\\n- 20 &= 5A \\\\\nA &= -4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $A=-4$ and $B=27$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096309 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c30dc350-d0c9-5a4d-87a0-4e2ebb1bb221" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Three-Term Partial Fraction Decomposition"}] :question/id #uuid "57242982-05a3-5104-9bf0-2c6f3350a781" :question/math-academy-id "e-12335" :question/problem "Given that\n$\\dfrac{3x^2-5x+8}{(x-3)^2(x+1)} = \\dfrac{A}{(x-3)} +\\dfrac{B}{(x-3)^2} + \\dfrac{C}{(x+1)}$,\nwhere $A$, $B$, and $C$ are real constants, calculate $A\\cdot B\\cdot C$." :question/worked-solution "First, we multiply the expression through by $(x-3)^2(x+1)$ to give\n\n$$\n3x^2-5x+8= A(x-3)(x+1)+B(x+1)+C(x-3)^2\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=3$ eliminates the $A$ and $C$ terms and allows us to solve for $B$. We get\n\n$$\n3(3)^{2} - 5(3) + 8 = B \\cdot 4⟹B = 5\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, plugging in $x=-1$ eliminates the $A$ and $B$ terms and allows us to solve for $C$. We get\n\n$$\n3(-1)^{2} - 5(-1) + 8 = C \\cdot (-4)^{2}⟹C = 1\n$$\n\nUnfortunately, there is no value of $x$ that we can substitute to eliminate both $B$ and $C$. However, using our results for $B$ and $C$ gives\n\n$$\n3x^2-5x+8= A(x-3)(x+1)+5(x+1)+1(x-3)^2\n$$\n\nand we can plug in any value of $x$ to solve for $A$. Let's choose the simplest number to plug in, $x=0$. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n8 &= A(0 - 3)(0 + 1) + 5(0 + 1) + C(0 - 3)^{2} \\\\\n8 &= -3A + 5 + 9 \\\\\n- 6 &= -3A \\\\\nA &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nA\\cdot B\\cdot C=2\\cdot 5\\cdot 1=10\n$$\n\nand the partial fraction decomposition is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3x^2-5x+8}{(x-3)^2(x+1)} = \\dfrac{2}{(x-3)} +\\dfrac{5}{(x-3)^2} + \\dfrac{1}{(x+1)}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096310 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e9c4ef2-0491-5b42-8679-4fe7dfbd7b00" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Partial Fraction Decomposition With Higher Order Factors"}] :question/id #uuid "17bbb974-0c14-5201-a28a-825f4ea385bd" :question/math-academy-id "e-12336" :question/problem "Given that\n\n$\\dfrac{1-2x}{(x+1)(x+2)^3}= \\dfrac{A}{x+1}+\\dfrac{B}{x+2} +\\dfrac{C}{(x+2)^2} + \\dfrac{D}{(x+2)^3}$,\n\nwhere $A$, $B$, $C$ and $D$ are real constants, determine the value of $AB + CD$." :question/worked-solution "Multiplying both sides of the given equality by $(x+1)(x+2)^3$ gives\n\n$$\n1 - 2x = A(x+2)^3 + B(x+1)(x+2)^2 + C(x+1)(x+2) + D(x+1)\n$$\n\nLetting $x = -2$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1 - 2(-2) &= A(-2 + 2)^{3} + B(-2 + 1)(-2 + 2)^{2} + C(-2 + 1)(-2 + 2) + D(-2 + 1) \\\\\n1 + 4 &= -D \\\\\nD &= -5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLetting $x = -1$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1 - 2(-1) &= A(-1 + 2)^{3} + B(-1 + 1)(-1 + 2)^{2} + C(-1 + 1)(-1 + 2) + D(-1 + 1) \\\\\n1 + 2 &= A \\\\\nA &= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLetting $x = 0$ and using our values for $A$ and $D$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1 - 2(0) &= 3(0 + 2)^{3} + B(0 + 1)(0 + 2)^{2} + C(0 + 1)(0 + 2) + (-5)(0 + 1) \\\\\n1 - 0 &= 24 + 4B + 2C - 5 \\\\\n2C &= -4B - 18 \\\\\nC &= -2B - 9.(1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, letting $x = 1$ and using our values for $A$ and $D$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1 - 2(1) &= A(1 + 2)^{3} + B(1 + 1)(1 + 2)^{2} + C(1 + 1)(1 + 2) + D(1 + 1) \\\\\n1 - 2 &= 81 + 18B + 6C - 10 \\\\\n6C &= -18B - 72 \\\\\nC &= -3B - 12.(2)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom $(1)$ and $(2)$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-2B - 9 &= -3B - 12 \\\\\nB &= -3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we substitute the value of $B$ into $(1)$ to get the value of $C{:}$\n\n$$\nC = -2(-3) - 9 = -3\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nAB + CD = 3(-3) + (-3)(-5) = 6\n$$"}]}
